TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2) – Space Mission Design","overview":"Students design a preliminary space mission benefiting Australia, covering the space segment: mission purpose, spacecraft count, users, launch site and vehicle, orbit and orbital elements, manoeuvres, ground control and communications, mission duration and end-of-life. Design may use STK, PIGI or approved software, supplemented by hand calculations, and is reported as a concise technical report with supporting screenshots and software files."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale - Include the following required information: • What is the spacecraft's primary mission? • Why is the mission needed? • How many spacecraft are used and why? • Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design - Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate - Include relevant screenshots and software files (in the main body of the report and/or appendix) - Include the following information: • Where it will launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? • What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? • Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? • Which ground control facilities will you use? • How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? • How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (eg. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"partial","where":"Mission Design"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"Executive Summary / Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R5","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Launch"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R8","met":"partial","where":"Orbit / Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R10","met":"yes","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R11","met":"yes","where":"Orbit / Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R12","met":"partial","where":"Image 1, Image 2, Image 4, Image 5"},{"id":"R13","met":"yes","where":"Contents / Bibliography"},{"id":"R14","met":"partial","where":"Image 1 / access table"},{"id":"R15","met":"no","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Orbit","issue":"Claim that a very high altitude would give low resolution 'which is also desirable' contradicts the mission requirement; no GSD/swath calculation justifies 600 km for a 250 km-wide reef."},{"where":"Orbit (COE table)","issue":"RAAN = 27.33 deg is said to indicate the launch facility location; RAAN is set by launch time/azimuth, not by the site."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"Reasoning that Kourou's 5.05 deg latitude gives two daily launch windows at ascending/descending node is incorrect; no discussion of the performance penalty of a 97.8 deg retrograde injection from Kourou."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Access table duplicates access 14/15 and has inconsistent numbering; no explicit passes-per-day or data-volume/link budget; Mt Pleasant is a radio telescope, suitability for TT&C/downlink not demonstrated."},{"where":"Orbit vs EOL","issue":"Earlier states the only manoeuvre is at EOL, later states the satellite has no thrust capability; EOL drag-sail sentence is truncated with no altitude/lifetime trade."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"Suggests solar panels could 'lengthen' life, but power generation is a baseline requirement, not an optional lifetime extension."},{"where":"Overall","issue":"No mass, power, data-rate or sensor specification; orbit selection not tied to actual instrument coverage requirement."}],"overall":"Sound, well-structured mission concept with genuine STK usage and clear justification of a single SSO satellite for GBR monitoring. Technical depth is limited: no swath/GSD or link budget, inverted resolution reasoning, unlabelled figures/tables, bare-URL references including Wikipedia, and internal inconsistencies on manoeuvres and EOL.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Mission purpose (coral reef/bleaching monitoring for the GBR), rationale with statistics and citations [1]-[4], spacecraft count justified, and users/operator identified. User discussion is hedged ('likely') and citations are bare URLs rather than full references, so not top-band.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The ZYZZsat however, does not require such constant coverage as the reef bleaching events occur over days and weeks. Thus, only one satellite is required."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The proposed satellite would likely be used by governmental and science agencies (e.g. CSIRO, BOM) for research and/or education."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":6.0,"scale":10.0,"points":36.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"All major items (Kourou/Vega E, SSO elements table, access table, 5-year life, drag-sail EOL) are covered with STK evidence and an inclination-altitude calculation. However, the resolution/swath logic is inverted, no GSD/link/mass budget supports the 600 km choice, and the EOL 'no thrusters' claim contradicts the earlier statement that a manoeuvre occurs at EOL.","evidence":[{"where":"Orbit","quote":"A very high altitude would yield low resolution images which is also desirable."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"An average contact duration of almost ten minutes was achieved, in addition to the already frequent amount of passes."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"However, the ZYZZsat is without thrust capability and so must use a ‘passive’ strategy."}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Accomplished","mark":7.0,"scale":10.0,"points":7.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Conclusions are tied to the design decisions (launch vehicle plus fallback, SSO illumination, Mt Pleasant station) and state the expected benefit. They remain qualitative and omit key numbers (contact statistics, element values), so low Accomplished.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A specific launch vehicle – the Vega E – has been selected although it has not been built yet. Thus, launch may need to be adjusted"}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":10.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Good overall structure (executive summary, TOC, headed sections, conclusion, bibliography) and readable prose. But figures are captioned without numbers, all tables are unlabelled, the EOL paragraph is truncated mid-sentence, and the bibliography is bare URLs including Wikipedia, so referencing does not meet the required standard.","evidence":[{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[6] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight. [Online]."},{"where":"Image 1","quote":""},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"It acts like a regular sail, utilising a large surface area to ‘ and is suitable for low-earth orbit altitudes"}]}]}
